 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "After a heavy wardrobe crashes down on Viktor Barinov, a doctor examines his ribs while Elena listens in. The physician confirms a hairline crack without bone displacement and asks how the injury happened.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Иди потом позавтракай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "У вас неполный перелом ребра.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Как я и предполагал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Вы же сказали, трещина.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Ну, это она и есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Радуйтесь. Как была получена травма?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Только шкаф упал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Большой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Килограммов сто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Даже побольше будет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Сто двадцать.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning.' Elena anchors the line on 'good', while 'morning' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Иди потом позавтракай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Go have breakfast later.' The grammar packs 'go' and 'have breakfast' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "У вас неполный перелом ребра.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You have an incomplete rib fracture.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'at' and finishing on 'rib'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Как я и предполагал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Just as I assumed.' the doctor brings out the contrast between 'how' and 'I' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Вы же сказали, трещина.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You said it was a crack.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'crack'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Ну, это она и есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, that is what it is.' Notice how Russian expresses 'come on' and 'there is' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Радуйтесь. Как была получена травма?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Be glad. How was the injury sustained?' In Russian, the idea of 'rejoice' flows naturally into 'injury' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Только шкаф упал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Only a wardrobe fell.' The grammar packs 'only' and 'fell' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Большой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A big one?' Notice how Russian expresses 'big' and 'big' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Килограммов сто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'About a hundred kilograms.' The grammar packs 'kilograms' and 'hundred' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Даже побольше будет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It is even heavier than that.' The meaning hinges on 'even' working together with 'will be' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Сто двадцать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'One hundred and twenty.' In Russian, the idea of 'hundred' flows naturally into 'twenty' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
